<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudyard Kipling</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1865">1865</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Joseph Rudyard Kipling</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Rudyard Kipling was an English journalist, novelist, poet and short-story writer, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, which inspired much of his work. His fiction includes The Jungle Book (1894), Kim (1901), the Just So Stories (1902) and &quot;The Man Who Would Be King&quot; (1888); his poems include &quot;Gunga Din&quot; (1890), &quot;The White Man's Burden&quot; (1899) and &quot;If—&quot; (1910). Among the most popular writers in the United Kingdom of his day, in 1907 he became the first English-language recipient of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Literature. His reputation has changed with the political climate, and critics such as Douglas Kerr see him as a controversial interpreter of how empire was experienced.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/rudyard-kipling.jpg" alt="Rudyard Kipling" width="330" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elliott &amp; Fry · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rudyard_Kipling_(portrait).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Kipling was born on 30 December 1865 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> to Alice Kipling, one of the four noted MacDonald sisters, and John Lockwood Kipling, a sculptor and pottery designer who taught at the newly founded Sir J. J. School of Art. At five he and his three-year-old sister Alice were taken to Southsea, Portsmouth, to live with a couple who boarded the children of British nationals abroad. For six years from October 1871 they lived with Captain Pryse Agar Holloway and his wife Sarah, in a house Kipling called &quot;the House of Desolation&quot;.</p><p>He was judged unable to win a scholarship to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oxford">Oxford</a>, so his father, then Principal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayo-college">Mayo College</a> of Art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahore">Lahore</a>, obtained him a post as assistant editor of the Civil and Military Gazette. From 1883 to 1889 he worked for newspapers in Lahore and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> and in 1886 published his first verse collection, Departmental Ditties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Kipling left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on 9 March 1889 and travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangon">Rangoon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, writing articles for The Pioneer later gathered as From Sea to Sea. In London he took rooms in Villiers Street, near Charing Cross. He later lived in Vermont, in Torquay, and from 1897 in Rottingdean in Sussex.</p><p>In 1897 and 1899 he wrote &quot;Recessional&quot; and &quot;The White Man's Burden&quot;. Some regarded them as anthems for dutiful empire-building, others as propaganda for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperialism">imperialism</a>, and still others saw irony and warnings in them. From 1898 until 1908 the family wintered in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, in a house on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cecil-rhodes">Cecil Rhodes</a>'s estate. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-boer-war">Second Boer War</a> he wrote verse for the British cause. From 1902 he lived at Bateman's in Burwash, East Sussex.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>His only son, John, was killed at the Battle of Loos in September 1915, aged 18. Kipling joined Sir Fabian Ware's Imperial War Graves Commission, choosing the phrase &quot;Their Name Liveth For Evermore&quot; for the Stones of Remembrance and &quot;Known unto God&quot; for the graves of unidentified servicemen.</p><p>He died at Middlesex Hospital on 18 January 1936, of a perforated duodenal ulcer. Bateman's passed to the National Trust after his wife's death in 1939 and is now a museum. The Wolf Cubs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-baden-powell-1st-baron-baden-powell">Robert Baden-Powell</a>'s Boy Scouts Association took themes and names from the Jungle Books.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rudyard Kipling” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rudyard Kipling</b>'s Puck of Pook's Hill (1906) contributed to the popular image of the &quot;Great Pict Wall&quot;, and W. H. Auden wrote a 1937 radio script and the poem &quot;Roman Wall Blues&quot; in the voice of a lonely Roman soldier stationed there.</blockquote><a href="/work/hadrians-wall">Hadrian's Wall · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
